# Networking

## Floating IPs

The allocation (`nemau_floating_ip`) and the association
(`nemau_floating_ip_association`) are separate resources, as in the API. This
lets you keep the address while replacing the machine behind it.

## IPv6

IPv6 is available in regions that have an IPv6 prefix, on floating IPs and on
subnets.

`ip_version = 6` on `nemau_floating_ip` allocates an address that the exit node
translates to the target's private IPv4 address. Nothing changes in the guest,
which sees IPv6 clients as addresses from the translation range. A target holds
at most one such address.

`ip_version = 6` on `nemau_subnet` makes the network dual-stack. The subnet
takes no `cidr` or `gateway_ip` (the platform assigns a /64 and reports it) and
requires an IPv4 subnet on the same network first. Instances created on the
network afterwards get a public IPv6 address and must have a security group.

```terraform
resource "nemau_subnet" "app_v6" {
  network_id = nemau_network.app.id
  name       = "app-v6"
  ip_version = 6
  depends_on = [nemau_subnet.app]
}
```

## Security group egress

A new security group enforces its egress rules and starts with two rules that
allow all outbound traffic, `0.0.0.0/0` and `::/0`. The group creates them
itself, so they are not Terraform resources until you import them. Declaring an
identical `nemau_security_group_rule` fails, because the group already has that
rule. An instance filters egress only when all of its groups do.

To restrict egress, import the two default rules and delete them, or keep one
of them. A rule imports as `<security_group_id>:<rule_id>`;
`nemau network security-group rules <group-id>` lists the rule ids.

```terraform
import {
  to = nemau_security_group_rule.web_egress_v4
  id = "<group-id>:<rule-id>"
}

resource "nemau_security_group_rule" "web_egress_v4" {
  security_group_id = nemau_security_group.web.id
  direction         = "egress"
  remote_ip_prefix  = "0.0.0.0/0"
}
```

Once the import is applied, removing the `resource` block deletes the rule.
Before you remove the default rules, add a rule of your own that keeps DNS
open, or name resolution stops. DHCP needs no rule.

`egress_filtering` reports which model the group uses. It is `false` on a group
created before egress filtering existed, typically an imported one: all
outbound traffic is allowed and its egress rules have no effect. Setting it to
`true` converts the group in place. The two allow-all rules are added, then the
outbound policy of its instances is recomputed, so traffic is unchanged until
those rules are removed or narrowed. The conversion is one-way: a plan that sets
it back to `false` is refused, and so is `false` on a new group.

```terraform
resource "nemau_security_group" "legacy" {
  name             = "legacy"
  egress_filtering = true # converts the group; this cannot be undone
}
```
